1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,300
What if the deepest mystery in all of quantum mechanics, like the underlying reason why physicists have been forced to use imaginary numbers to describe the universe for the last century, is actually just a math typo we made back in the 1920s?

2
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:34,180
I mean, it sounds borderline heretical when you put it exactly like that. But that is genuinely the frontier of theoretical physics right now. Because for 100 years, the imaginary unit, you know, the square root of negative one, which we denote with the letter I, has just been sitting right there in the core equations of reality.

3
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:35,820
Right, like in Schrodinger's equation.

4
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:44,000
Exactly. It's how we calculate the fundamental states of particles. And for a century, physicists have largely just, well, shrugged and accepted it.

5
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,060
Because the math works, right? The predictions are accurate to 10 decimal places, so why question the alphabet we use to write the equation?

6
00:00:51,060 --> 00:01:03,920
But questioning that alphabet is exactly our mission for this deep dive. Because today, we are sinking our teeth into a highly detailed, honestly, incredibly dense, hot-off-the-presses, 2026 manuscript.

7
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:10,980
It's titled Unistochastic Quantum Mechanics, and it's written by an independent researcher who publishes under the name Flyxion.

8
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:20,980
Yeah, and the goal of this manuscript is nothing less than tearing down quantum mechanics to the absolute studs and rebuilding it from the ground up.

9
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:21,280
Right.

10
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:29,740
And crucially, doing it without assuming any of the textbook rules are true to begin with. Like, this isn't about taking Schrodinger's equation as a given.

11
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:33,300
So we're not just accepting the standard textbook as gospel here.

12
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:43,180
Not at all. The methodology here is to start with the most basic, undeniable, logical rules of how probability works, and then aggressively push those rules until they break.

13
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:51,580
Because, you know, we are looking for the exact moment where classical math shatters. That fracture line is where the quantum structure of the universe is basically forced to emerge.

14
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:54,960
Okay, so we have a clear blueprint for where we're taking you today.

15
00:01:55,780 --> 00:02:02,040
We'll start in a completely classical world, just simple statistics, absolutely no quantum weirdness at all.

16
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:06,360
Then we are going to push that system until we find a mathematical contradiction.

17
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:08,000
Yep, the breaking point.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:17,060
Right. We'll explore why complex numbers seem to perfectly patch that contradiction, especially when two different systems try to, you know, talk to each other.

19
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:26,100
And then at the very end, we are going to drop a massive 2026 plot twist about whether the universe actually needed those imaginary numbers to begin with.

20
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:29,880
But to appreciate that twist, we have to start at the absolute foundation.

21
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:35,700
I mean, we need to define the bare admissible rules of probabilistic reality.

22
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:36,760
Before any physics.

23
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:42,840
Right, before physics. Before we talk about electrons or photons or spin or gravity, we need to look at what information actually is.

24
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:47,680
Let's unpack this bare foundation then, because the manuscript starts with something called the probability simplex.

25
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:51,000
And I really appreciate how stripped down this concept is.

26
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:55,680
Imagine you have a finite set of perfectly distinguishable alternatives.

27
00:02:56,420 --> 00:02:57,100
Like rolling a die.

28
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:59,880
Yeah, like the six faces of a regular die.

29
00:03:00,420 --> 00:03:02,320
Or it could be a simple coin flip.

30
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:07,520
Or even a detector in a lab that either clicks or stays completely silent.

31
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:13,700
So a state of knowledge about which of those alternatives is true is, well, it's just a probability distribution.

32
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:18,860
It's a list of non-negative numbers that add up to exactly one or, you know, 100%.

33
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:22,920
And if you picture this geometrically, it forms a shape called a simplex.

34
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:24,700
Okay, so how do we visualize that simplex?

35
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:27,260
Let's say you have three possible outcomes.

36
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:28,660
You could just draw a triangle.

37
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:34,680
The three corners or the vertices of that triangle, they represent states of absolute certainty.

38
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:37,880
Like, you know for a fact the dial landed on a one.

39
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:39,700
You are 100% certain.

40
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:46,400
But if I'm not certain, if I'm, say, just guessing, my state of knowledge exists somewhere inside the body of that triangle, right?

41
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:46,940
Exactly.

42
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:49,900
Any point inside that shape is a convex combination.

43
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:50,520
It's a mixture.

44
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:56,800
So maybe you are 50% sure it's outcome A, 25% outcome B, and 25% outcome C.

45
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:00,040
That point loosed geometrically inside the simplex.

46
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:02,380
Okay, so I have my list of probabilities.

47
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:04,460
I have my point inside the triangle.

48
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:06,540
Now I want to do something physical.

49
00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:10,280
I want to roll the die or shake the box or just let time pass.

50
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:11,740
Like, I want to change the probabilities.

51
00:04:12,560 --> 00:04:15,800
In the math, this is called a stochastic map, right?

52
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:18,480
Yeah, a stochastic map is simply a mathematical transformation.

53
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:26,240
You can think of it as a matrix that grabs your current point in the probability simplex and moves it to a new valid point.

54
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:27,980
And there are rules for that movement.

55
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:28,240
Right.

56
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:33,400
The only fundamental rule of a stochastic map is that it cannot break the laws of probability.

57
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:38,480
It'd have to preserve the fact that your total certainty sums to 1.

58
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Like, no matter how you transform the system, the columns of this matrix have to add up to 1.

59
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:46,860
And obviously, you can't suddenly have a negative probability.

60
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:48,200
Yeah, that makes intuitive sense.

61
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:52,120
You can't have, like, a negative 10% chance of a coin landing on heads.

62
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:52,820
That's absurd.

63
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,420
But Flyxion almost immediately narrows our focus here.

64
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:57,420
We don't just look at any stochastic math.

65
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:03,520
We look at a very specific restriction called a bistochastic matrix or a doubly stochastic matrix.

66
00:05:03,580 --> 00:05:04,200
Yes, exactly.

67
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:08,980
Walk me through the difference there because this seems to be the engine for the entire first act of the manuscript.

68
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:12,880
The distinction is subtle, but it's mathematically profound.

69
00:05:14,140 --> 00:05:18,500
A regular stochastic matrix just requires its columns to sum to 1.

70
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:23,840
A bistochastic matrix demands that both the columns and the rows sum to 1.

71
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,220
Okay, but why does that matter?

72
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:30,420
Like, what does forcing the rows to equal 1 actually do to our system physically?

73
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:33,240
It creates a perfectly balanced transformation.

74
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:44,100
So think about what happens if you feed the ultimate state of uncertainty, you know, the completely uniform distribution where every single outcome is equally likely, into a bistochastic matrix.

75
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:44,380
Right.

76
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:49,980
Because the rows also sum to 1, the matrix spits that exact same uniform distribution right back out.

77
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:51,400
It doesn't skew the board.

78
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:53,260
It doesn't favor one outcome over another.

79
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:53,780
Oh, I see.

80
00:05:53,860 --> 00:05:56,020
It's like a casino game that is actually fair.

81
00:05:56,140 --> 00:06:01,260
If you walk in with absolutely no information, the game doesn't secretly push you toward losing.

82
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:08,840
Let's push that idea even further, using the Burkhoff von Neumann theorem, which the manuscript relies on really heavily here.

83
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:13,640
This theorem provides a beautiful geometric way to understand these balanced matrices.

84
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:14,700
What does it say?

85
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:24,140
It basically proves that any bistochastic matrix, no matter how messy or fuzzy it looks on paper, is actually just a mixture of simple, definitive permutations.

86
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:27,380
Define permutation for me in this specific context.

87
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:31,060
A permutation is a strict, definitive relabeling of reality.

88
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:33,400
Outcome 1 becomes outcome 2.

89
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:35,320
Outcome 2 becomes outcome 3.

90
00:06:35,780 --> 00:06:38,360
There was no fuzziness, no rolling the dice.

91
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:39,940
It's absolute certainty.

92
00:06:40,040 --> 00:06:41,380
So it's totally deterministic.

93
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:41,860
Right.

94
00:06:42,220 --> 00:06:46,360
If you know exactly where you started, a permutation tells you exactly where you end up.

95
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:53,080
The Burkhoff von Neumann theorem says that the space of all possible bistochastic matrices forms a shape, like a polytope.

96
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:56,220
And the sharp corners of that shape are the pure permutations.

97
00:06:56,960 --> 00:07:02,220
Every fuzzy probabilistic transition inside the shape is literally just a weighted average of those sharp corners.

98
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:05,840
Man, this reminds me so much of shuffling a deck of cards.

99
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:08,620
Like, let's say you buy a brand new deck.

100
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:09,980
It is perfectly ordered.

101
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:13,000
You know the Ace of Spades is, say, the 40th card down.

102
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:15,360
That is a state of absolute certainty.

103
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:18,000
You are at a corner of our probability simplex.

104
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:18,480
Exactly.

105
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:26,680
Now, if you do a perfect deterministic cut of the deck, just taking half the cards and putting them on the bottom, that is a permutation.

106
00:07:27,020 --> 00:07:29,060
And you still know exactly where the Ace of Spades is.

107
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:30,980
Your certainty hasn't degraded at all.

108
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:32,180
It just shifted its label.

109
00:07:32,340 --> 00:07:32,580
Right.

110
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:35,020
But when I do a messy Rufal Shuffle...

111
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:35,980
Ah, yes.

112
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:37,760
That is a bistochastic map.

113
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:47,160
I am essentially mixing together a bunch of different deterministic permutations based on the random physical probabilities of how my thumbs let the cards in or leave.

114
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:51,440
And notice what happens to your knowledge as you keep doing that messy Rufal Shuffle.

115
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:55,940
You started with the Ace of Spades at 100% probability in one specific slot.

116
00:07:56,160 --> 00:08:00,400
After one shuffle, maybe it's spread across five possible slots.

117
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:01,680
So my certainty is dropping.

118
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:05,080
After 10 shuffles, you have absolutely no idea where it is.

119
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:11,020
Your probability distribution has flattened out to be completely uniform across all 52 cards.

120
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:13,580
Which introduces the concept of majorization.

121
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:17,200
I found this part so fascinating when reading the source.

122
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:23,000
Majorization is essentially the rigorous mathematical language for that flattening out we just described.

123
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:31,380
When you apply a bistochastic matrix to a probability distribution, the resulting distribution is always majorized by the original one.

124
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:33,080
What does that mean operationally?

125
00:08:33,920 --> 00:08:37,100
Operationally, it means the system is forced to become more uniform.

126
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:39,520
It's kind of like a Robin Hood effect for data.

127
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:47,140
The math intrinsically takes probability away from the most likely outcomes and distributes it to the least likely ones.

128
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:52,820
And because it is mathematically guaranteed that this bistochastic shuffling always flattens the distribution,

129
00:08:53,260 --> 00:08:59,280
it mathematically proves that Shannon entropy, which is the measure of disorder or missing information, can never decrease.

130
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:02,660
This is a really vital methodological point in the manuscript.

131
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:04,040
Look at what we just did there.

132
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:06,280
We proved that entropy must increase.

133
00:09:06,580 --> 00:09:08,280
But we didn't even mention thermodynamics.

134
00:09:08,620 --> 00:09:13,560
We didn't talk about heat or gas particles in a box or the arrow of time.

135
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:14,060
Exactly.

136
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:21,600
The increase of entropy here is a purely structural geometric consequence of how bistochastic matrices work.

137
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:26,760
It is a fundamental feature of the admissible rules of mixing information.

138
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:29,320
Okay, so I am following the logic perfectly here.

139
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:37,540
We have classical probabilities, we have bistochastic matrices acting like messy card shuffles, entropy goes up, it all makes total sense.

140
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:44,660
So if standard probability works so perfectly, why did physicists ever need to invent imaginary numbers?

141
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:48,680
Like where does this beautiful intuitive classical math actually break?

142
00:09:49,220 --> 00:09:51,840
Because right now I'm very comfortable in my classical casino.

143
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,800
Well that comfort is exactly what the manuscript is about to shatter.

144
00:09:54,800 --> 00:10:00,180
If we want to find the fracture line where classical reality breaks, we need to introduce the concept of lifts.

145
00:10:00,520 --> 00:10:02,980
Okay, let's transition into the first major break in the math.

146
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:10,880
Because what blew my mind in the source material is that classical probability doesn't fail in some, you know, multi-billion dollar particle accelerator.

147
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:14,720
It fails simply by increasing our number of choices from two to three.

148
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:17,660
It is stunningly elegant when you lay it all out.

149
00:10:18,220 --> 00:10:19,980
So let's define what we mean by a lift.

150
00:10:19,980 --> 00:10:24,500
In quantum mechanics, we don't calculate probabilities directly.

151
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:25,980
We calculate amplitudes.

152
00:10:26,780 --> 00:10:26,920
Right.

153
00:10:27,220 --> 00:10:29,320
Amplitudes are deeper mathematical objects.

154
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:33,520
They can be negative or they can be complex numbers with an imaginary part.

155
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:42,120
To get from the hidden quantum amplitude to the physical probability that you actually measure on a screen, you have to square the amplitude.

156
00:10:42,120 --> 00:10:48,040
So if I have a 50% chance of something happening, the underlying amplitude would be the square root of 0.5.

157
00:10:48,120 --> 00:10:48,540
Exactly.

158
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:51,020
So a lift is basically a reverse engineering question.

159
00:10:51,540 --> 00:11:03,360
If I hand you a valid bistochastic matrix of probabilities, you can ask, hey, can I find a matrix of underlying amplitudes that, when I square every individual entry, perfectly recreates my matrix of probabilities?

160
00:11:03,700 --> 00:11:07,720
You are trying to lift the probability matrix back up into an amplitude matrix.

161
00:11:07,720 --> 00:11:11,620
Yes, and the manuscript outlines two different tools we can use to build these lifts.

162
00:11:11,820 --> 00:11:14,680
We have orthostochastic lifts and unistochastic lifts.

163
00:11:14,880 --> 00:11:15,880
Break down the difference for me.

164
00:11:16,220 --> 00:11:21,340
An orthostochastic lift restricts you to using only real numbers, just regular numbers.

165
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:29,020
Geometrically, this requires finding a real orthogonal matrix, which is basically just a rotation in standard multidimensional space.

166
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:30,540
Okay, and the unistochastic one.

167
00:11:30,900 --> 00:11:35,240
A unistochastic lift allows you to use complex numbers, you know, numbers with that imaginary eye.

168
00:11:35,240 --> 00:11:40,800
This requires finding a unitary matrix, which is the standard workhorse of textbook quantum mechanics.

169
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:43,460
So it's basically a battle of the mathematical fields.

170
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:46,040
Real number math versus complex number math.

171
00:11:46,220 --> 00:11:50,140
Flyxion sets the stage with the n equals 2 degeneracy theorem.

172
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:56,220
Tell me what happens when our universe only has two possible outcomes, like a simple coin flip.

173
00:11:56,840 --> 00:11:57,420
Heads or tails?

174
00:11:57,420 --> 00:12:04,960
When you constrain a system to only two states, n equals 2, an incredible mathematical coincidence occurs.

175
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:14,940
The set of all possible real-numbered orthostochastic matrices and the set of all possible complex-numbered unistochastic matrices are perfectly identical.

176
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:16,400
Wait, they overlap exactly.

177
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:17,020
100%.

178
00:12:17,020 --> 00:12:18,300
Meaning what, practically speaking?

179
00:12:18,300 --> 00:12:31,020
It means if you have a 2x2 probability transition that can be recreated by squaring the entries of a complex matrix, it can also be perfectly recreated by squaring the entries of a purely real matrix.

180
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:36,080
At the level of a single two-state system, they are mathematically indistinguishable.

181
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:44,040
So if the entire universe only ever consisted of two state systems, we might never have stumbled into imaginary numbers at all.

182
00:12:44,160 --> 00:12:47,520
Like, real numbers were doing the job perfectly well.

183
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:50,540
You would have absolutely no reason to invent complex numbers.

184
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:58,580
A single 2x2 transition matrix simply cannot tell you whether the underlying scalar field of reality is real or complex.

185
00:12:59,260 --> 00:13:00,400
The math is degenerate.

186
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:05,480
But then we get to the n is greater than or equal to 3 incompleteness theorem.

187
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:11,620
The moment we add a third option, a three-sided die, a traffic light with three colors, the math just completely separates.

188
00:13:11,740 --> 00:13:12,620
It doesn't just separate.

189
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:14,240
The real numbers structurally fail.

190
00:13:14,760 --> 00:13:20,000
The manuscript proves this using a very specific famous mathematical object as a counterexample.

191
00:13:20,280 --> 00:13:23,660
It's called the 3x3 discrete Fourier transform matrix.

192
00:13:24,120 --> 00:13:26,460
Now, we don't need to get bogged down in the name.

193
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:32,240
All you really need to know is that if you take this specific 3x3 complex unitary matrix

194
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:36,460
and you take the absolute value squared of every single entry,

195
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:41,380
you generate a 3x3 matrix where every single entry is exactly one-third.

196
00:13:41,380 --> 00:13:46,440
Okay, so it creates a perfectly uniform 3x3 bistochastic matrix.

197
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:46,880
Yes.

198
00:13:47,460 --> 00:13:50,740
And because we literally just built it from a complex unitary matrix,

199
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:56,320
we know for an absolute fact that this uniform probability matrix has a complex lift.

200
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:58,160
It is un-stochastic.

201
00:13:58,760 --> 00:14:00,520
Complex numbers can handle it just fine.

202
00:14:00,780 --> 00:14:06,460
But then Flyxion asks, can we build that exact same uniform one-third matrix using only real numbers?

203
00:14:06,700 --> 00:14:06,880
Right.

204
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,760
Let's walk through what that would actually demand.

205
00:14:08,940 --> 00:14:11,200
To build it using a real orthogonal matrix,

206
00:14:11,460 --> 00:14:17,320
we need to find a 3x3 grid of real numbers where every single number, when squared, equals one-third.

207
00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:22,260
Which means every single entry has to be either positive or negative 1 over the square root of 3.

208
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:22,660
Exactly.

209
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:25,140
But it also has to be an orthogonal matrix.

210
00:14:25,300 --> 00:14:25,400
Yeah.

211
00:14:25,620 --> 00:14:30,240
In linear algebra, orthogonal means that every row has to be perfectly perpendicular to every other row.

212
00:14:30,440 --> 00:14:31,820
So they don't interfere with each other.

213
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:32,040
Right.

214
00:14:32,140 --> 00:14:37,260
If you multiply the matching entries of any two rows together and add them up, they have to equal zero.

215
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:40,940
When you run the math on those specific constraints,

216
00:14:41,620 --> 00:14:45,380
demanding both the one-third squaring and the perpendicular rows,

217
00:14:45,380 --> 00:14:53,840
it turns out you are mathematically requiring the existence of what is called a 3x3 Hadamard matrix.

218
00:14:53,980 --> 00:14:55,300
I've actually seen that term before.

219
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:57,400
Unpack a Hadamard matrix for me.

220
00:14:57,480 --> 00:15:02,320
A Hadamard matrix is a square grid filled only with positive ones and negative ones,

221
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:05,400
arranged in such a way that all the rows are mutually orthogonal.

222
00:15:06,140 --> 00:15:07,720
They're mathematically beautiful structures.

223
00:15:07,940 --> 00:15:11,580
They're used heavily in error-correcting codes and classical signal processing.

224
00:15:11,580 --> 00:15:14,820
You can easily build a 2x2 Hadamard matrix.

225
00:15:15,180 --> 00:15:17,200
You can build a 4x4 and 8x8.

226
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:18,580
But we need a 3x3.

227
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:21,140
And here is the brutal reality of mathematics.

228
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:24,800
A 3x3 Hadamard matrix does not exist.

229
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:27,540
It is a mathematical impossibility.

230
00:15:27,620 --> 00:15:28,200
Wait, really?

231
00:15:28,520 --> 00:15:28,740
Yeah.

232
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:29,480
Think about it.

233
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:32,560
A 3x3 grid has nine squares.

234
00:15:33,220 --> 00:15:35,680
Each square can be a plus one or a minus one.

235
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:39,940
That means there are 512 possible ways to fill that grid.

236
00:15:39,940 --> 00:15:43,660
You can write all 512 of them down on a piece of paper.

237
00:15:44,200 --> 00:15:45,560
You can test every single one.

238
00:15:46,200 --> 00:15:50,180
Not a single one of them will have rows that are all orthogonal to each other.

239
00:15:50,400 --> 00:15:52,060
They simply do not exist.

240
00:15:52,180 --> 00:15:52,660
Wait, wait.

241
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:54,700
Let me just make sure I'm really getting the weight of this.

242
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:57,860
A completely uniform probability transition,

243
00:15:58,320 --> 00:16:01,700
just a 33% chance of moving to any of the three states,

244
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:06,740
is one of the most basic, admissible, classical probabilities imaginable.

245
00:16:06,740 --> 00:16:10,320
And you are saying that if the universe uses underlying amplitudes,

246
00:16:10,560 --> 00:16:16,060
it is literally impossible to use real number to describe that simple 33% transition.

247
00:16:16,760 --> 00:16:19,160
The real numbers simply lack the structural flexibility.

248
00:16:19,740 --> 00:16:24,520
They are not expressive enough to capture the required geometry of a 3c uniform transition.

249
00:16:24,520 --> 00:16:29,400
Man, it's like saying a two-way intersection works perfectly fine with regular traffic laws.

250
00:16:29,940 --> 00:16:33,120
You have stop signs, you have right-of-way, everything flows beautifully.

251
00:16:33,660 --> 00:16:38,540
But the moment you try to build a three-way roundabout, the laws of physics break down.

252
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:42,080
The cars suddenly run out of physical space to yield to each other,

253
00:16:42,220 --> 00:16:47,560
and the only way to prevent a crash is to force the cars to travel through a higher invisible dimension.

254
00:16:47,560 --> 00:16:51,560
It sounds completely absurd, but you are saying the math is ironclad.

255
00:16:52,580 --> 00:16:54,940
The roundabout is a fantastic analogy, actually.

256
00:16:55,180 --> 00:16:57,620
The real numbers run out of topological room to maneuver.

257
00:16:58,260 --> 00:17:02,480
They cannot bend enough to accommodate three perpendicular directions simultaneously

258
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:04,920
while maintaining that uniform distribution.

259
00:17:05,620 --> 00:17:08,420
So this rigorously establishes that at n equals 3,

260
00:17:08,900 --> 00:17:13,880
complex numbers are structurally necessary to realize every admissible classical probability matrix.

261
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:16,060
But the manuscript doesn't stop there.

262
00:17:16,060 --> 00:17:19,100
Because a stubborn physicist might look at that and say,

263
00:17:19,240 --> 00:17:22,240
well, fine, the 3x3 roundabout is broken.

264
00:17:22,600 --> 00:17:25,360
I will just refuse to study three-state systems.

265
00:17:25,740 --> 00:17:29,940
I will strictly stay in the n equals 2 world, the two-way intersection,

266
00:17:30,260 --> 00:17:34,420
where you already prove that real numbers and complex numbers agree perfectly.

267
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:38,360
And that takes us directly into what the manuscript calls the gluing problem.

268
00:17:39,080 --> 00:17:43,120
Because Flyxion mathematically corners the stubborn physicist here.

269
00:17:43,120 --> 00:17:48,420
The book demonstrates that even if you stick to the safe, happy world of two outcome systems,

270
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:53,140
if you chain enough of those safe transitions together, real numbers still break down.

271
00:17:53,300 --> 00:17:55,700
This is the concept of the composition obstruction.

272
00:17:56,140 --> 00:17:56,940
Walk me through this.

273
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:00,100
How does chaining things together destroy the real numbers?

274
00:18:00,100 --> 00:18:05,040
Let's imagine you have three different measurement contexts for a single two-state system.

275
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:09,940
Think of them as, say, three different angles you can orient a polarizing filter to measure a single photon.

276
00:18:10,540 --> 00:18:13,440
We'll call them context Z, context X, and context Y.

277
00:18:13,620 --> 00:18:20,220
Okay, so I can set up my lab to measure the transition probability of a photon going from context Z to context X.

278
00:18:20,360 --> 00:18:23,920
And then I can measure a separate transition going from context X to context Y.

279
00:18:23,920 --> 00:18:29,240
Right, and you can find a perfectly valid real-numbered amplitude matrix for the Z to X transition

280
00:18:29,240 --> 00:18:33,560
and a perfectly valid real lift for the X to Y transition.

281
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:36,580
Because we are strictly looking at N equals 2,

282
00:18:36,840 --> 00:18:41,260
we know from the degeneracy theorem that those real lifts absolutely exist.

283
00:18:41,460 --> 00:18:43,880
So where does the obstruction actually happen?

284
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:47,840
The obstruction happens when you try to calculate the direct transition from Z to Y

285
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:50,000
by chaining the intermediate steps together.

286
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:53,020
If you compose the physical probabilities directly,

287
00:18:53,440 --> 00:18:56,200
just multiplying the statistics like you would in a classical casino,

288
00:18:56,500 --> 00:18:57,500
you get the wrong answer.

289
00:18:57,780 --> 00:18:59,960
You lose crucial physical information.

290
00:19:00,360 --> 00:19:01,180
What kind of information?

291
00:19:01,380 --> 00:19:04,340
Specifically, you lose the quantum interference across terms.

292
00:19:04,920 --> 00:19:10,240
The act of projecting down into pure probability destroys the exact composition law.

293
00:19:10,380 --> 00:19:14,680
So, to get the right physical answer, we can't multiply the probabilities.

294
00:19:15,280 --> 00:19:18,380
We have to multiply the underlying amplitude matrices first,

295
00:19:18,380 --> 00:19:21,520
let the positive and negative numbers interfere with each other,

296
00:19:21,700 --> 00:19:25,000
and then square the final result to get the new probability.

297
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:27,180
You've hit on the core tension there perfectly.

298
00:19:27,460 --> 00:19:28,740
We have to compose the lifts.

299
00:19:29,320 --> 00:19:31,580
And this leads to the concept of context graphs.

300
00:19:32,180 --> 00:19:34,280
Imagine drawing this out on a whiteboard.

301
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:35,480
You draw a triangle.

302
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:41,040
The three corners of the triangle are our three measurement contexts, Z, X, and Y.

303
00:19:41,100 --> 00:19:41,400
Got it.

304
00:19:41,500 --> 00:19:43,900
The three lines connecting the corners, the edges,

305
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:47,580
are the transition probabilities between those contexts.

306
00:19:47,580 --> 00:19:50,200
And this is where the source draws a massive distinction

307
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:52,920
between an edgewise truth and a global truth.

308
00:19:53,420 --> 00:19:54,500
Let's really dig into this,

309
00:19:54,540 --> 00:19:57,880
because it feels like the philosophical heart of the whole first half of the book.

310
00:19:58,440 --> 00:19:59,340
It absolutely is.

311
00:19:59,520 --> 00:20:01,840
Let's say you have a graph of measurement contexts,

312
00:20:02,180 --> 00:20:04,380
but instead of triangle, it's shaped like a tree.

313
00:20:04,980 --> 00:20:08,300
It has branches, but no closed loops, no cycles.

314
00:20:08,380 --> 00:20:09,220
Okay, a tree structure.

315
00:20:09,500 --> 00:20:11,600
The math proves that on a tree graph,

316
00:20:11,940 --> 00:20:16,720
you can always find a consistent set of real numbered amplitudes for all your measurements.

317
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:20,260
You just start at the trunk, assign it a real frame,

318
00:20:20,680 --> 00:20:23,640
and propagate your real numbered lists outwards down the branches.

319
00:20:24,280 --> 00:20:26,700
Because the branches never loop back and cross each other,

320
00:20:26,940 --> 00:20:28,320
you never run into a contradiction.

321
00:20:29,520 --> 00:20:33,720
Edgewise, real admissibility guarantees global real admissibility.

322
00:20:34,060 --> 00:20:35,880
But a whiteboard drawing isn't a tree.

323
00:20:36,280 --> 00:20:37,100
It's a triangle.

324
00:20:37,360 --> 00:20:38,400
It's a closed loop.

325
00:20:38,680 --> 00:20:41,720
It has what the source calls a cycle rank of one.

326
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:43,820
And that one single loop ruins everything.

327
00:20:43,820 --> 00:20:47,360
The triangle gluing theorem in the manuscript proves that when you have a cycle,

328
00:20:47,780 --> 00:20:51,460
edgewise real admissibility does not imply global real admissibility.

329
00:20:51,700 --> 00:20:54,180
Meaning, I can look at any single line on my triangle,

330
00:20:54,280 --> 00:20:56,020
and it works perfectly with real numbers.

331
00:20:56,260 --> 00:20:58,380
But I can't make the whole triangle work at once.

332
00:20:58,640 --> 00:20:59,000
Exactly.

333
00:20:59,340 --> 00:21:01,700
You can have transition probabilities on all three edges

334
00:21:01,700 --> 00:21:05,760
that look perfectly fine and have valid real numbered lifts individually.

335
00:21:06,240 --> 00:21:09,800
But when you try to assign a single consistent global reality,

336
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:14,040
like a single real numbered framework to Z, X, and Y simultaneously,

337
00:21:14,380 --> 00:21:18,920
that successfully reproduces all three transition edges without contradiction,

338
00:21:19,720 --> 00:21:21,260
it is mathematically impossible.

339
00:21:21,500 --> 00:21:24,220
The specific example Slick-Sugent uses to prove this

340
00:21:24,220 --> 00:21:27,020
is the real mutually unbiased basis obstruction,

341
00:21:27,540 --> 00:21:28,900
the MUB obstruction.

342
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:30,840
Let's unpack that jargon.

343
00:21:31,220 --> 00:21:34,720
What does mutually unbiased actually mean operationally in a lab?

344
00:21:34,860 --> 00:21:37,300
Mutually unbiased basically means the measurement contexts

345
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:39,760
are as fundamentally different as they can possibly be.

346
00:21:40,080 --> 00:21:41,980
It is maximum incompatibility.

347
00:21:42,380 --> 00:21:45,760
If you are 100% certain of a particle's state in context Z,

348
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,100
you are maximally uncertain about its state in context X.

349
00:21:49,280 --> 00:21:52,220
Oh, like it acts exactly like a 50-50 coin flip.

350
00:21:52,400 --> 00:21:52,600
Right.

351
00:21:52,680 --> 00:21:55,060
This is the essence of Heisenberg's uncertainty principle.

352
00:21:55,260 --> 00:21:56,600
If you know the position perfectly,

353
00:21:56,920 --> 00:21:59,040
the momentum is completely mutually unbiased.

354
00:21:59,120 --> 00:21:59,920
It's totally fuzzy.

355
00:21:59,920 --> 00:22:03,220
So, we set up our triangle so that all three edges,

356
00:22:03,440 --> 00:22:07,120
Z to X, X to Y, and Y to Z, are mutually unbiased.

357
00:22:07,580 --> 00:22:10,080
Every transition is a 50-50 coin flip.

358
00:22:10,580 --> 00:22:14,220
And if you force that specific, highly symmetric scenario,

359
00:22:14,920 --> 00:22:17,560
the real number math creates a legal contradiction.

360
00:22:18,320 --> 00:22:21,100
To make the real amplitudes balance around the loop,

361
00:22:21,480 --> 00:22:25,200
the geometry demands that a certain angle equation must hold true.

362
00:22:25,200 --> 00:22:30,740
It basically demands that an angle of 45 degrees must be geometrically equivalent

363
00:22:30,740 --> 00:22:32,900
to either 0 degrees or 90 degrees.

364
00:22:33,020 --> 00:22:34,980
Which is obviously a catastrophic contradiction.

365
00:22:35,140 --> 00:22:36,640
45 does not equal 90.

366
00:22:36,820 --> 00:22:38,480
It is a total structural collapse.

367
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:42,320
I actually really liked an analogy you hinted at earlier when we were reviewing the notes.

368
00:22:42,780 --> 00:22:43,900
The Escher staircase.

369
00:22:44,160 --> 00:22:44,460
Yes.

370
00:22:45,240 --> 00:22:48,640
Visualizing this as an MC Escher drawing really makes it click for me.

371
00:22:48,760 --> 00:22:51,400
If you look at any single corner of an Escher staircase,

372
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:54,780
which represents one edge of our context graph, one transition,

373
00:22:54,960 --> 00:22:56,060
it looks completely normal.

374
00:22:56,260 --> 00:22:56,980
It obeys gravity.

375
00:22:57,120 --> 00:22:58,200
It obeys the laws of perspective.

376
00:22:58,600 --> 00:23:02,440
You could build that single corner out of real wood in a real carpenter shop.

377
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:03,900
It is edgewise admissible.

378
00:23:04,100 --> 00:23:05,680
The local geometry is flawless.

379
00:23:06,180 --> 00:23:10,700
But when you zoom out and your eye follows the stairs around the entire loop,

380
00:23:11,120 --> 00:23:12,820
the geometry contradicts itself.

381
00:23:13,160 --> 00:23:16,820
You are perpetually walking upwards, taking positive steps,

382
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:20,440
but you somehow end up on the exact same floor where you started.

383
00:23:20,440 --> 00:23:20,960
Yes.

384
00:23:20,960 --> 00:23:24,140
The real numbers fail to glue together to close the loop.

385
00:23:24,260 --> 00:23:26,240
You absolutely need the extra phase information,

386
00:23:26,420 --> 00:23:30,920
the extra dimension of complex numbers to twist the staircase through a higher dimension

387
00:23:30,920 --> 00:23:33,320
and close the cycle without breaking the map.

388
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:36,160
The Escher staircase captures the essence perfectly.

389
00:23:36,900 --> 00:23:38,120
The contradiction isn't local.

390
00:23:38,540 --> 00:23:40,380
The real numbers aren't failing at the corners.

391
00:23:40,580 --> 00:23:41,980
They are failing topologically.

392
00:23:42,300 --> 00:23:43,560
They fail globally.

393
00:23:44,140 --> 00:23:48,240
And what's fascinating is how meticulously Flyxion audits this failure.

394
00:23:48,760 --> 00:23:50,480
The triangle is the minimal witness.

395
00:23:50,480 --> 00:23:50,880
Yeah.

396
00:23:50,880 --> 00:23:55,340
It rigorously proves that real numbers are fundamentally incapable of supporting cycle

397
00:23:55,340 --> 00:23:59,080
consistency, even for the simplest two-state systems in the universe.

398
00:23:59,080 --> 00:24:03,680
So after the roundabout failure at n equals 3 and the Escher staircase failure at n equals 2,

399
00:24:03,760 --> 00:24:06,400
I have to ask, is this the final nail in the coffin?

400
00:24:06,540 --> 00:24:10,300
Like, is this absolute proof that real numbers are dead and the universe must be complex?

401
00:24:10,300 --> 00:24:12,620
It is a devastating blow.

402
00:24:12,620 --> 00:24:19,120
But it only rules out real numbers for a single isolated system experiencing transitions around a loop.

403
00:24:19,640 --> 00:24:26,500
If we want to truly test the scalar field of reality to see if the universe is fundamentally real or complex,

404
00:24:26,980 --> 00:24:30,020
we can't just look at one particle talking to itself.

405
00:24:30,020 --> 00:24:35,640
We have to look at what happens when two completely separate, independent systems interact with each other.

406
00:24:35,880 --> 00:24:39,300
And that introduces section 4, bringing systems together.

407
00:24:39,980 --> 00:24:43,540
This is where we test the third axis of the manuscript's architecture,

408
00:24:43,900 --> 00:24:48,220
and it introduces a concept that I found deeply profound, local tomography.

409
00:24:48,620 --> 00:24:52,340
Define local tomography for me, because this seems to be the linchpin of the whole debate.

410
00:24:52,480 --> 00:24:55,280
Local tomography is a foundational operational principle.

411
00:24:55,900 --> 00:24:59,120
It is essentially the mathematical formalization of reductionism.

412
00:24:59,560 --> 00:25:02,940
It's the idea that if you have a joint system made of two independent parts,

413
00:25:02,940 --> 00:25:07,460
let's say Alice has a particle in her lab on Earth and Bob has a particle in his lab on Mars.

414
00:25:08,200 --> 00:25:14,680
The state of the total combined system should be perfectly 100% describable by Alice locally testing her system,

415
00:25:14,680 --> 00:25:19,160
Bob locally testing his system, and then just multiplying their lists of data together.

416
00:25:19,500 --> 00:25:22,960
In other words, if I want to know everything there is to know about the joint system,

417
00:25:23,180 --> 00:25:26,860
I just need to ask Alice everything she can possibly know about her side,

418
00:25:26,980 --> 00:25:29,940
and ask Bob everything he can possibly know about his side.

419
00:25:30,280 --> 00:25:34,740
There shouldn't be any spooky, holistic parameters that only reveal themselves

420
00:25:34,740 --> 00:25:39,680
when you put the two particles in the exact same room and measure them together.

421
00:25:40,060 --> 00:25:40,540
Exactly.

422
00:25:40,540 --> 00:25:46,140
Local tomography demands that the whole is exactly equal to the sum of its local parts.

423
00:25:46,680 --> 00:25:48,180
Now, we have to be very careful here.

424
00:25:48,620 --> 00:25:53,980
The manuscript explicitly flags that local tomography is an assumption.

425
00:25:54,520 --> 00:25:56,260
It is not a guaranteed law of nature.

426
00:25:56,620 --> 00:26:00,860
But it is an incredibly intuitive, physically reasonable assumption

427
00:26:00,860 --> 00:26:03,720
that matches almost all of our classical experience.

428
00:26:03,800 --> 00:26:03,920
Right.

429
00:26:03,980 --> 00:26:07,620
If I have two cars, I don't need to crash them together to know their individual weights and speeds.

430
00:26:07,860 --> 00:26:08,380
Precisely.

431
00:26:08,380 --> 00:26:10,660
So, the manuscript runs a stress test.

432
00:26:10,980 --> 00:26:16,080
It asks, how do our different number systems fare when we demand that local tomography must be true?

433
00:26:16,440 --> 00:26:19,760
And the way Flyxion does this is brilliantly simple.

434
00:26:20,540 --> 00:26:22,040
It doesn't require deep calculus.

435
00:26:22,300 --> 00:26:24,520
It literally just requires counting dimensions.

436
00:26:25,160 --> 00:26:30,120
Counting how many parameters, how many dials it takes to completely describe a state space.

437
00:26:30,440 --> 00:26:33,680
Walk me through this dimension counting because the results are staggering.

438
00:26:34,240 --> 00:26:37,040
Let's start with the reigning champion, complex numbers.

439
00:26:37,160 --> 00:26:37,660
Let's start there.

440
00:26:37,660 --> 00:26:46,440
If Alice has a generic quantum system of dimension D, her complex state space requires a specific number of parameters to fully describe.

441
00:26:47,220 --> 00:26:49,500
Mathematically, it requires the dimension squared.

442
00:26:50,120 --> 00:26:58,780
So, if Alice has a basic two-level system, a quivit, it takes two squared or four independent parameters to fully describe her complex state space.

443
00:26:58,780 --> 00:27:03,280
And Bob also has a two-level quivit, so his state space also takes four parameters.

444
00:27:03,500 --> 00:27:03,640
Right.

445
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:06,160
Okay, Alice has four dials, Bob has four dials.

446
00:27:06,240 --> 00:27:10,300
Now, what happens when we put them together into a joint four-level composite system?

447
00:27:10,440 --> 00:27:11,340
We run the same formula.

448
00:27:11,780 --> 00:27:16,640
A joint four-level complex system requires four squared, which is exactly 16 parameters.

449
00:27:16,840 --> 00:27:21,600
And if you look at our local dials, Alice's four times Bob's four perfectly equals 16.

450
00:27:21,760 --> 00:27:22,160
Oh, wow.

451
00:27:22,160 --> 00:27:24,580
The dimensions match flawlessly.

452
00:27:25,700 --> 00:27:30,000
Every single parameter of the joint system can be accounted for by the local measurements.

453
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:36,400
The math of combining two complex systems perfectly matches our physical intuition of local independence.

454
00:27:36,780 --> 00:27:38,780
It's the Goldilocks mathematical field.

455
00:27:39,060 --> 00:27:40,320
Everything is just right.

456
00:27:41,020 --> 00:27:44,400
But what happens when we force the universe to use real numbers?

457
00:27:44,860 --> 00:27:46,600
We strip away the imaginary eye.

458
00:27:46,900 --> 00:27:48,380
What happens to the dials?

459
00:27:48,380 --> 00:27:49,380
The math changes.

460
00:27:50,280 --> 00:27:55,700
If we use strictly real numbers, a two-level symmetric system requires only three parameters.

461
00:27:56,200 --> 00:27:58,220
So Alice has three local dials to play with.

462
00:27:58,560 --> 00:28:00,780
Bob has a two-level system, so he has three dials.

463
00:28:01,100 --> 00:28:04,580
Locally, they could discover three times three, which is nine total independent parameters.

464
00:28:04,760 --> 00:28:06,140
Okay, nine local parameters.

465
00:28:06,380 --> 00:28:09,340
But what does the math say a joint four-level real system requires?

466
00:28:09,540 --> 00:28:12,920
A joint four-level real symmetric system requires ten parameters.

467
00:28:13,160 --> 00:28:13,420
Ten?

468
00:28:13,580 --> 00:28:15,040
